WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.917
Limiter algorithms shape both protection and user experience.

2
00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:09.439
A fixed window limiter counts requests during a fixed period and resets on a schedule.

3
00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:17.979
It is straightforward to reason about, but traffic near a window boundary can feel uneven because one period ends and another begins.

4
00:00:17.979 --> 00:00:26.583
A sliding window limiter smooths behavior by dividing time into smaller segments and accounting for recent activity across the window.

5
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:30.371
This can reduce the sharp boundary effect of fixed windows.

6
00:00:30.371 --> 00:00:37.563
It is often a better fit when teams want a time-based request cap that feels more consistent to users over time.

7
00:00:37.563 --> 00:00:40.645
A token bucket limiter allows controlled bursts.

8
00:00:40.645 --> 00:00:44.241
Tokens replenish over time, and requests consume tokens.

9
00:00:44.241 --> 00:00:51.432
This model can be useful when an application should tolerate short bursts while still enforcing an average rate.

10
00:00:51.432 --> 00:00:59.458
It should be configured around realistic endpoint cost and backend capacity, not around an arbitrary desire to permit bursts.

11
00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:03.889
A concurrency limiter is different from a time-based request limiter.

12
00:01:03.889 --> 00:01:08.833
Instead of counting requests over a period, it caps simultaneous active work.

13
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:17.565
That can be useful for expensive operations that tie up CPU, database connections, storage calls, or downstream services while they run.

14
00:01:17.565 --> 00:01:22.831
A concurrency limit may protect a backend even when the request count is not high.

15
00:01:22.831 --> 00:01:27.454
Queue limits and queue ordering affect user experience and resource use.

16
00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:34.645
Queueing can smooth short bursts, but queued requests still consume memory, wait time, and operational capacity.

17
00:01:34.645 --> 00:01:38.690
A queue that is too large can make failures slow and confusing.

18
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:44.212
A queue that is too small can reject short bursts that the system could safely absorb.

19
00:01:44.212 --> 00:01:47.038
The queue should be intentional and bounded.

20
00:01:47.038 --> 00:01:53.844
Algorithm choice should be based on user experience, resource cost, burst tolerance, and backend capacity.

21
00:01:53.844 --> 00:01:58.339
A cheap read endpoint may need a different model from a report export.

22
00:01:58.339 --> 00:02:05.337
Authentication, upload, search, and tenant-wide operations may deserve stricter or more specialized policies.

23
00:02:05.337 --> 00:02:09.639
The right limiter is the one that matches the work being protected.
